<file path="README.md">
# free_search

**A web retriever for AI agents. Give it a URL and a question — it reads the page and returns the answer, with no search API, no external service, and no AI inside the retriever itself.**

The AI supplies one or more candidate URLs. `free_search` fetches the pages, navigates the site's structure, fixes wrong or invented URLs automatically, and hands back ranked text passages the AI can use to answer the user.

---

## Contents

- [Setup](#setup)
- [How it works](#how-it-works)
- [AI integrations](#ai-integrations)
- [MCP server](#mcp-server)
- [Controlling which sites are allowed](#controlling-which-sites-are-allowed)
- [Retrieval depth](#retrieval-depth)
- [Rate limits](#rate-limits)
- [Optional features](#optional-features)
- [Limitations](#limitations)
- [Full specification](#full-specification)

---

## Setup

**Requirements:** Node.js 20 or newer, pnpm 9 or newer.

```bash
# clone and install
pnpm install

# build
pnpm build

# run tests
pnpm test
```

**To use in your own project:**

```bash
npm install web-retriever
# or
pnpm add web-retriever
```

**Quick example:**

```ts
import { retrieve } from "web-retriever";

const result = await retrieve({
  url: "https://example.com/docs/getting-started",
  query: "how do I configure authentication",
});

// result.status   — "success", "partial", "not_found", "blocked", ...
// result.evidence — ranked text passages from the page
```

---

## How it works

1. The AI provides a URL (or a list of up to 10 candidates in order of preference).
2. `free_search` validates the URL, checks the site's `robots.txt`, and fetches the page.
3. If the URL is wrong or invented, the retriever checks the site's sitemap and link structure to find the closest real match.
4. The page content is parsed (HTML, JSON, XML, PDF, etc.) and split into passages.
5. Passages are scored against the query locally — no external model needed.
6. The top-ranked passages are returned as evidence for the AI to use.

One tool call is normally enough. The AI does not need to loop.

### What it reads

HTML, JSON, XML, RSS and Atom feeds, plain text, Markdown, CSV, and optionally PDF.

### What it protects against

- Requests to private or internal network addresses (SSRF)
- Redirect loops and DNS rebinding attacks
- Oversized responses and compressed payloads designed to exhaust memory
- Malformed HTML and XML

---

## AI integrations

Each AI provider has its own sub-import so the core stays lean.

### OpenAI

```ts
import { webRetrieverTool, handleToolCall } from "web-retriever/openai";

// Pass the tool definition to the API
const response = await openai.chat.completions.create({
  model: "gpt-4o",
  tools: [webRetrieverTool],
  messages: [...],
});

// Handle the tool call when the model requests it
const content = await handleToolCall(JSON.parse(toolCall.function.arguments));
messages.push({ role: "tool", tool_call_id: toolCall.id, content });
```

### Anthropic Claude

```ts
import { webRetrieverTool, handleToolUse } from "web-retriever/anthropic";

const message = await anthropic.messages.create({
  model: "claude-3-5-sonnet-20241022",
  tools: [webRetrieverTool],
  messages: [...],
});

const content = await handleToolUse(block.input);
messages.push({ role: "user", content: [{ type: "tool_result", tool_use_id: block.id, content }] });
```

### Google Gemini

```ts
import { webRetrieverFunctionDeclaration, handleFunctionCall } from "web-retriever/gemini";

const model = genAI.getGenerativeModel({
  model: "gemini-1.5-pro",
  tools: [{ functionDeclarations: [webRetrieverFunctionDeclaration] }],
});

const result = await handleFunctionCall(part.functionCall.args);
```

All three adapters accept up to 10 candidate URLs per call. If the first URL fails, the next one is tried automatically without a second round-trip to the AI.

---

## MCP server

`free_search` can run as a standalone tool server using the [Model Context Protocol](https://modelcontextprotocol.io). Register it once and any compatible AI host (Claude Desktop, Cursor, Continue, and others) can call `web_retrieve` as a tool.

**Run directly:**

```bash
node ./node_modules/web-retriever/dist/integrations/mcp.js
```

**Register in your MCP host config:**

```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "free_search": {
      "command": "node",
      "args": ["./node_modules/web-retriever/dist/integrations/mcp.js"]
    }
  }
}
```

The tool accepts these parameters:

| Parameter | Required | Description |
|---|---|---|
| `urls` | Yes | One to ten URLs to try, most likely first |
| `query` | Yes | What you want to find on the page |
| `mode` | No | `fast`, `balanced` (default), or `deep` |
| `enablePdf` | No | Set to `true` to read PDF files (default: `false`) |

---

## Controlling which sites are allowed

### Allow-list — restrict to specific domains

Pass `allowDomains` to limit the retriever to only those domains. Requests to any other domain are blocked, including URLs the AI invents. This is the recommended approach when you want `free_search` to act as a focused index over a known set of sites — the AI cannot stray outside it.

```ts
await retrieve({
  url: "https://docs.mycompany.com/api",
  query: "authentication",
  allowDomains: ["docs.mycompany.com", "api.mycompany.com"],
});
```

### Deny-list — block specific domains

Pass `denyDomains` to block certain domains while allowing everything else.

```ts
await retrieve({
  url: "https://some-site.com/page",
  query: "pricing",
  denyDomains: ["ads.tracker.com"],
});
```

**Which to use:** The allow-list is safer — it prevents the AI from generating plausible-sounding URLs on sites you never intended to fetch. The deny-list is better when you need broad access and only want to exclude a handful of known domains.

---

## Retrieval depth

Control how thoroughly the retriever searches before returning:

| Mode | What it does |
|---|---|
| `fast` | Fetches the given page only, plus one sitemap candidate. Fewest requests. |
| `balanced` | Default. Tries up to 6 pages, stopping as soon as enough evidence is found. |
| `deep` | Uses the full request budget before returning. Best results on large sites. |

```ts
await retrieve({ url, query, mode: "fast" });
await retrieve({ url, query, mode: "balanced" }); // default
await retrieve({ url, query, mode: "deep" });
```

---

## Rate limits

`free_search` is polite by default. It waits at least 500 ms between requests to the same domain and respects `Crawl-delay` from the site's `robots.txt`.

All limits can be tuned per call:

```ts
await retrieve({
  url: "https://example.com",
  query: "...",
  maxPages: 3,              // max pages to fetch (default: 6)
  maxInternalRequests: 5,   // total request budget including robots/sitemaps (default: 8)
  timeoutMs: 5_000,         // per-request timeout in ms (default: 8000)
  maxResponseBytes: 524288, // max response size in bytes (default: 2 MiB)
});
```

To change the minimum delay between requests to the same domain, edit this line in [`packages/web-retriever/src/fetch/rate-limiter.ts`](packages/web-retriever/src/fetch/rate-limiter.ts):

```ts
const MIN_INTER_REQUEST_MS = 500; // change to your preferred value
```

All defaults are in [`packages/web-retriever/src/core/defaults.ts`](packages/web-retriever/src/core/defaults.ts).

---

## Optional features

### PDF reading

Disabled by default. Enable it per call:

```ts
await retrieve({ url, query, enablePdf: true });
```

Requires the `unpdf` package, which is already listed as an optional dependency.

### OCR for scanned documents

When a PDF contains no text layer (a scan), the retriever signals that OCR is needed but does not perform it itself. You can inject an OCR provider to handle this. A ready-made `web-retriever-ocr` adapter is planned for a future release.

### Browser rendering for JavaScript-heavy pages

Some pages load all their content with JavaScript and have no readable HTML source. For these, a separate `web-retriever-browser` package can be added:

```ts
import { createBrowserFetcher } from "web-retriever-browser";

await retrieve({
  url,
  query,
  enableBrowser: true,
  fetchOverride: createBrowserFetcher(),
});
```

Note: this requires a persistent Node.js process and is not compatible with serverless environments.

### External search as a URL source

If you want the retriever to pull candidate URLs from a search engine rather than relying on the AI's suggestions, you can plug in a discovery provider. A `web-retriever-searxng` community adapter is available for self-hosted SearXNG instances:

```ts
import { createSearxngProvider } from "web-retriever-searxng";

await retrieve({
  url,
  query,
  discoveryProvider: createSearxngProvider("https://your-searxng-instance"),
});
```

### Custom cache

By default, responses are cached in memory for the duration of the process. You can provide a persistent cache (Redis, filesystem, etc.) by implementing the `CacheProvider` interface:

```ts
await retrieve({ url, query, cache: myRedisCache });
```

---

## Limitations

**It is not a search engine.** It reads pages you point it at. It does not crawl the web or maintain an index.

**One site per call.** Each call works within a single website. To retrieve from multiple sites, make multiple calls.

**JavaScript-only pages.** If a page renders entirely in the browser and has no readable HTML source, the core cannot extract content from it. The browser adapter handles this but requires a non-serverless environment.

**No login or session support.** The retriever does not handle authentication, cookies, or any form of logged-in access.

**Scanned PDFs return no text** without an OCR provider.

**Blocked by robots.txt.** Sites that disallow crawling will return a `blocked` status. You can override this with `respectRobots: false`, but do so carefully.

**It returns what pages say, not what is true.** Evidence is ranked by relevance to your query, not by accuracy or credibility.

